+## Findings (new, dedup-checked)
+
+### F-1 · CRITICAL — Sign-in is broken on the Pages deploy: passkey endpoint 405, password auth request never fires
+- **Evidence:** `POST https://supremeai-studio.pages.dev/api/auth/passkey/login/options` → **HTTP 405** (empty body), observed 3× (page-load init + submit clicks). Clicking **Sign in** with a *valid-format* email+password (`test.user@example.com` / `ValidPassword123!`) sends **no authentication request at all** — no `auth/login`, no session call; URL stays `/login`. Only the broken passkey call fires. Raw toasts shown to the user: **"HTTP Error 405:"** (twice) and persistent alert **"Failed to initialize biometric login🔍 Check connection"** plus a red **"Check connection"** badge.
+- **Impact:** The entire authenticated funnel is unreachable from the studio. The error copy is also wrong — it tells users to "check connection" when their connection is fine; the endpoint itself does not accept POST on this deploy (Cloudflare Pages static 405 — no Pages Function at `/api/auth/*`). Note the inconsistency: working API calls (`/api/config/public`, `/v1/chat/completions`, `/health`) all go to `supremeai-api-ulw0.onrender.com`, while auth calls go same-origin to the Pages host — a base-URL/configuration mismatch is the most likely root cause.
+- **Smallest safe fix (suggestion):** Point the auth client's base URL at the API gateway (same origin pattern as the working `/api/config/*` calls) or provide a Pages Function for `/api/auth/*`; until fixed, stop auto-initializing passkey on page load so users are not greeted with a failure badge, and reword the error to describe the actual failure. Fixer should confirm which of the two base-URL targets is intended (repo fixer lane, not guessable from outside).
+- **Regression test:** Live probe asserting `POST /api/auth/passkey/login/options` is not 405; e2e test asserting a valid-credential submit fires exactly one auth request (not zero) and no raw `HTTP Error <code>:` toast is ever user-visible.
+- **why:** Login is the monetization/retention gate; every new user hitting "Get started" lands here. It is broken in production *right now* and the misleading "Check connection" copy actively sends users away to debug their own network.
+- **alternatives_rejected:** (1) Reporting only the cosmetic toast copy — rejected because the missing auth request is the real defect, the copy is a symptom. (2) Fixing it directly from here — rejected: base-URL intent (gateway vs Pages Function) is an in-repo decision the human-eye mandate explicitly leaves to the fixer lane; guessing would risk shipping a wrong-surface patch.
+
+### F-2 · HIGH — Provider-internal grounding URL rendered inside the assistant chat bubble
+- **Evidence:** Guest chat, question "What is SupremeAI in one sentence?" → assistant bubble (model tag `SupremeAITurbo (w-gp)`) ends with a raw line: `http://googleusercontent.com/lmdx_content/NlPBSgQOcjBxUsetltrXAuPzXLMJqigPtQWWydbiqGcvWvUlmqCJIphxTJnBIhGKGCLVpfrTqPthrAVdQyyTUnqBpRJzTijPnhpyUKjwbISQhXfBvekXSzPqhzpEQSXEO34339` (full string captured from DOM; screenshot `chat-response.png`). Confirmed 1 text-node hit in the rendered message.
+- **Impact:** Leaks provider-internal grounding metadata into user-facing content, looks broken/unprofessional, and the unbreakable 184-char token overflows its bubble on narrow viewports. Users can click/hover it — it is not a usable citation.
+- **Smallest safe fix (suggestion):** Sanitize assistant message content before render (strip `googleusercontent.com/lmdx_content/*` and similar provider-internal URL patterns) — one guard in the message-rendering path of the studio, independent of backend engine choices (deliberately kept render-side so it covers all providers, and does not touch the server grounding lane discussed in #1740).
+- **Regression test:** Unit test on the sanitizer: message containing an `lmdx_content` URL renders with zero such URLs and preserves the prose; e2e assertion that no rendered assistant bubble contains `googleusercontent`.
+- **why:** Every grounded reply on every model that returns this metadata will leak it; it is user-visible in the primary product surface (guest chat is the top-of-funnel journey per #1783).
+- **alternatives_rejected:** (1) Server-side stripping only — rejected as sole fix: server lanes are per-provider and #1740 is already reworking that lane; a render-side guard is provider-agnostic and immediately verifiable. (2) CSS `overflow:hidden` — hides nothing but the symptom; the string remains in DOM (copyable, screen-reader-readable).
+
+### F-3 · MEDIUM — Muted body text fails WCAG AA: 4.23:1 on the dark surface
+- **Evidence:** Computed style measurement (proper OKLCH→sRGB conversion, alpha-composited against effective background `rgb(2,6,24)`): suggestion-card description text foreground `rgb(98,116,142)` (slate-500-class muted token), 14 px / 400 → **contrast ratio 4.23:1** (AA normal-text bar is 4.5:1). Screenshots: `mobile-chat.png`, `chat-page.png` (the four "Help me …" card descriptions). Headings/nav on the same surface measure 18.4:1 — the token itself is the outlier, not the surface.
+- **Impact:** All muted secondary copy across the app (card descriptions, captions using the same token) is below AA for the most common reading sizes; accessibility regression risk on any audit.
+- **Smallest safe fix (suggestion):** Shift the existing muted-foreground design token one step lighter in the current palette (slate-400-class ≈ `rgb(148,163,184)` ≈ 7.9:1 on this surface) — a token-level change, no component redesign, no hardcoded per-component colors (respects the existing design-token rule).
+- **Regression test:** Add a design-token contrast pin in the studio test suite asserting muted-foreground-on-surface ≥ 4.5:1 (CI-cheap, no browser needed).
+- **why:** It is the default body-text color of the empty-state cards every guest sees on first chat visit; it fails the same accessibility bar the role mandates.
+- **alternatives_rejected:** (1) Darkening the surface instead — rejected: changes the whole visual system for one token. (2) Per-component inline overrides — rejected: violates the no-hardcoded-color rule and creates drift.
+
+### F-4 · MEDIUM — Mobile chat: model selector visually clipped off the right edge at 375 px
+- **Evidence:** 375×812 viewport, `/chat`: page-level scroll is fine (`scrollWidth == clientWidth == 375`), but the model `select.flex-1` renders **398 px wide with right edge at 590 px** — visually cut mid-word ("Supreme Auto — Best b…") with no ellipsis, inside the clipped toolbar row (`mobile-chat.png`). The row (Chat/JARVIS toggle + selector) does not shrink or wrap on narrow screens.
+- **Impact:** On iPhones (375/390 px class) users cannot read the current model name and may not realize the selector is interactive; truncated UI reads as a rendering bug.
+- **Smallest safe fix (suggestion):** Constrain the select within the flex row (`min-w-0` + `text-overflow: ellipsis` on the control, or let the selector wrap under the toggle at `sm` breakpoint) using the existing toolbar component classes.
+- **Regression test:** Playwright viewports 375/390/768: assert `select.getBoundingClientRect().right <= viewport.width` and `scrollWidth == clientWidth` on `/chat`.
+- **why:** Chat is the primary funnel (#1783 verified the landing side but not the chat toolbar at 375 px); #1783's 390 px horizontal-scroll check cannot catch this because the page does not scroll — the element is simply clipped.
+- **alternatives_rejected:** (1) Horizontal scroll for the toolbar row — rejected: introduces exactly the mobile scroll regression #1783 just verified as fixed. (2) Shortening model labels globally — changes product naming everywhere for a one-container CSS fix.
+
+### F-5 · LOW — Login form: no client-side validation feedback (overlaps F-1 surface, separate defect)
+- **Evidence:** Inputs have `required: false`, `aria-invalid` stays `null` after submitting `not-an-email` + 5-char password; no inline error, no `aria-describedby` link; browser-native `type=email` validation is bypassed by the JS submit. The only feedback is the raw 405 toast from F-1.
+- **Smallest safe fix (suggestion):** Validate email format + non-empty password before submit; set `aria-invalid` + `aria-describedby` to an inline error (existing form-error styling).
+- **Regression test:** Component test: submit invalid email → inline error visible, `aria-invalid="true"`, zero network calls.
+- **why:** Even after F-1 is fixed, invalid input will go straight to the wire; screen readers currently get no error announcement either.
+- **alternatives_rejected:** Server-only validation — worse UX and still silent given the current broken endpoint.
+
+### F-6 · LOW — Chat error copy uses internal jargon; no retry affordance
+- **Evidence:** Offline simulation (browser `offline` mode, real keyboard send): error bubble renders "Webhook relay is currently offline or unreachable. (network error (timeout, DNS or CORS))" + tag `[webhook-offline]` (`chat-offline2.png`). The message is graceful and the state handling itself **PASSES**, but "Webhook relay" is internal vocabulary for a guest user, and there is no retry button — the user must retype/resend manually.
+- **Smallest safe fix (suggestion):** Reword to user language ("We couldn't reach the AI service — check your connection and try again") and add a retry affordance reusing the existing send action.
+- **Regression test:** Offline e2e: error state shows, retry resends the same message once connectivity returns.
+- **why:** Error states are trust-critical copy; the detection/props of the current implementation are good and worth keeping.
+- **alternatives_rejected:** Auto-retry with backoff — silently resending paid model calls on flaky networks without user consent is a product decision, not a bug fix; flagging here instead.
